---
title: P09 Step 7 on SQLite - the same race, refused by the triggers of db/sqlite/init.sql (observed through the app)
---
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  actor Tool as tools/race_http.py
  participant M as Maya's request (connection 1)
  participant DB as SQLite file: ONE writer at a time
  participant O as Omar's request (connection 2, any instance)

  Tool->>M: POST /reservations
  M->>DB: SELECT EXISTS overlap (no transaction yet)
  DB-->>M: false
  Tool->>O: POST /reservations
  O->>DB: SELECT EXISTS overlap
  DB-->>O: false
  M->>DB: UPDATE reservation_ids ... RETURNING -> 101 (takes the write lock)
  M->>DB: INSERT R101 - trigger reservations_no_overlap_insert looks: nothing, row written
  O->>DB: UPDATE reservation_ids ... (waits: Maya holds the write lock, busy timeout 5 s)
  M->>DB: COMMIT (lock released)
  DB-->>O: 102 (the lock is now Omar's, he sees Maya's committed row)
  O->>DB: INSERT R102
  DB--xO: trigger RAISE(ABORT) "reservations_no_overlap: ..." (SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_TRIGGER)
  Note over O: only the INSERT is backed out, SqlReservationStore -> OverlapRejected,<br/>COMMIT keeps number 102 used
  M-->>Tool: 201 Created R101
  O-->>Tool: 409 Conflict Unavailable
  Note over DB: The trigger's look-then-write is atomic only because SQLite lets one<br/>connection write at a time. The same trigger in PostgreSQL let both<br/>rows in (observed) - there the constraint is needed.
